    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 1]                 ->  true\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4]                 ->  false\nnums = [1, 1, 1, 3, 3, 4, 3, 2, 4]  ->  true"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: sort first, then scan"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Many questions are hard on a jumbled array and easy on a sorted one. **Sorting puts related values next to each other**: equal values become neighbours, the closest pair of numbers sits side by side, and intervals line up by start time so overlaps are adjacent. After an O(n log n) sort, a single O(n) scan comparing neighbours (or tracking a running value) finishes the job."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The cost to remember: sorting is O(n log n) and either modifies the input or needs a copy. When a hash map can answer the question in O(n), it's faster — but sorting needs no extra memory if you may reorder the input, and it's the only easy route when the question is about **order** (closest, overlapping, the h-th largest). The questions in this chapter all follow this shape."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: compare every pair"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function containsDuplicate(nums) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n      if (nums[i] === nums[j]) return true;\n    }\n  }\n  return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def contains_duplicate(nums):\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):\n            if nums[i] == nums[j]:\n                return True\n    return False"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n            if (nums[i] == nums[j]) return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n        for (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n            if (nums[i] == nums[j]) return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n²), space O(1).** About n²/2 comparisons — too slow for 10⁵ values."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Sort, then compare neighbours"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "contains-duplicate",
      "caption": "After sorting, a duplicate must sit right next to its twin."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function containsDuplicate(nums) {\n  const sorted = [...nums].sort((a, b) => a - b);   // equal values end up side by side\n  for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {\n    if (sorted[i] === sorted[i - 1]) return true;   // a value equal to its neighbour\n  }\n  return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def contains_duplicate(nums):\n    s = sorted(nums)                      # equal values end up side by side\n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        if s[i] == s[i - 1]:              # a value equal to its neighbour\n            return True\n    return False"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    int[] sorted = nums.clone();\n    Arrays.sort(sorted);                          // equal values end up side by side\n    for (int i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {\n        if (sorted[i] == sorted[i - 1]) return true;   // a value equal to its neighbour\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> sorted = nums;\n    sort(sorted.begin(), sorted.end());           // equal values end up side by side\n    for (size_t i = 1; i < sorted.size(); i++) {\n        if (sorted[i] == sorted[i - 1]) return true;   // a value equal to its neighbour\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n log n), space O(n)** for the copy — or **O(1)** extra if you may sort `nums` itself."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. A hash set of values seen"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function containsDuplicate(nums) {\n  const seen = new Set();\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    if (seen.has(x)) return true;                 // seen before: a duplicate\n    seen.add(x);\n  }\n  return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def contains_duplicate(nums):\n    seen = set()\n    for x in nums:\n        if x in seen:                     # seen before: a duplicate\n            return True\n        seen.add(x)\n    return False"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (!seen.add(x)) return true;            // add() is false when it was already there\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    unordered_set<int> seen;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (!seen.insert(x).second) return true;  // already there: a duplicate\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(n).** The fastest, at the price of a set as big as the input."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Brute force: compare every pair",
          "time": "O(n²)",
          "timeWhy": "Every value is compared with every later one: about **n²/2** pairs.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just two indexes."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Sort, then compare neighbours",
          "time": "O(n log n)",
          "timeWhy": "The **sort** dominates; after it, equal values sit **side by side** for one O(n) scan.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Sorting `nums` **in place** (O(n) if you sort a copy)."
        },
        {
          "approach": "A hash set of values seen",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "One pass with an **O(1)** set lookup per value.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The set can hold **every value**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Mention both 2 and 3: the set is faster, sorting saves memory. Interviewers like hearing the trade-off."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Empty or one value** — false.",
        "**The duplicates far apart** — `[1, 2, 3, 1]`; sorting brings them together.",
        "**Negative numbers** — fine for both, but in JavaScript remember the numeric comparator.",
        "**Sorting the caller's array** — `nums.sort()` changes their data; copy first if that matters."
      ]
    },
